# Agent society - who is available and when to use them

This file lists the agents maintained in this suite. It is not exhaustive:
agents may exist outside it and can be discovered through the harness, for
example the installed-workflow and agent listings; the user may request
recruiting an agent that is not listed. Ask the user only when a routing
choice materially affects the requested outcome and cannot be resolved from
the available documentation.

Conversational agents load a start_chat and answer in chat; some carry their
own tooling. Inference workflows are workflow directories whose orchestration
is described in their README; they run stages instead of acting as a single
persona. All of them import doctrine for the shared rules.

Routing in one line:

- Reasonably sized request: Planned, the staged pipeline.
- Single bounded task: go straight to the specialist who owns it.
- Scout-stack code or tooling to build or extend: ScoutCoder.
- Research execution on that tooling: Worker.
- Verification of a consequential result: Critic.
- Introspection over recorded sessions: ChatAnalyst.

# Conversational agents

## User
Liaison and reporting agent. Restates a raw user request into a
self-contained instruction set, and turns validated outputs into the final
report. Runs the request and ask stages of Planned.
Use for: intake normalization and final synthesis.
Not for: executing tools, searching, or solving the task.

## Searcher
Targeted-search agent producing a short search brief, or NO_SEARCH_NEEDED
when no search is required; prefers local documentation and repository
files over the web. In Planned the brief is one of the three reports that
feed the work stage.
Use for: bounded evidence gathering.
Not for: design decisions or implementation suggestions.

## Planner
Plan producer for Planned's plan stage: turns the restated request, plus
the search brief when present, into one practical plan; plans from the
restatement alone when the search stage is off. Produces a single path,
not alternatives.
Use for: turning a restated request into an executable plan.
Not for: executing the plan, which belongs to the work stage.

## Manager
Controller of a budgeted loop: decides what happens next, who does it, with
what context, and when to stop; does not implement. Inside Planned its
context is exactly the three upstream reports. Schedules ScoutCoder when
supporting code infrastructure must be extended, and Worker when that
infrastructure is used for scientific research; recruits Critic for
verification gates.
Use for: routing ambiguous or multi-step work across specialists.
Not for: direct implementation.

## Consultant
Strategic inference point of the suite: expensive and valued for judgment,
not labor.
Use for: ambiguous or high-risk decisions, architecture and design
strategy, scientific methodology, rethinking after surprising evidence.
Not for: repository exploration, repetitive tools, ordinary coding, routine
coordination, generic best-practice essays.

## Critic
Verifier of other agents' work products, not a second executor: scores
results against the request, plan, repository, and evidence, and returns
PASS, NEEDS_WORK, or BLOCKED with the smallest next action. Contrasts with
ChatAnalyst, which examines sessions rather than work products.
Use for: verification gates on consequential results, branch advice.
Not for: redoing the investigation or fixing defects.

# Conversational agents with an associated tool or workflow

## Worker
Research executor: runs concrete tasks on the infrastructure ScoutCoder
builds, carrying Workspace and Cortex tooling plus any domain-specific
tooling, and produces evidence that cites result addresses rather than
transcribed numbers. Default for concrete tasks that are not explicitly
another agent's responsibility.
Use for: concrete tasks producing tangible artifacts.
Not for: deciding what the next step should be when a plan exists; works
one well-defined step at a time and asks for a better step-level task when
the request is too coarse.

## ChatAnalyst
Introspective analyst of recorded work sessions in Scout-AI chat format;
follows provenance across chats, ask receipts, and sidecars to find usage
patterns, tool friction, and failure modes, and can report into live
sessions.
Use for: post-mortems and evidence gathering about how agents actually
behaved.
Not for: implementing changes to harness or instructions, only reporting.

## ScoutCoder
Ruby and Scout framework development specialist with documentation lookup
over the scout-gear, scout-essentials, scout-camp, scout-ai, and scout-rig
repos plus installed workflows; its help tools are also introduced into
other agents' chats.
Use for: coding in the Scout stack, framework questions, project
understanding, workflow development, extending shared tooling, semantic
entity definitions.
Not for: general web research or non-Scout coding conventions.

# Inference workflows

## Planned
Staged pipeline and standard entry for reasonably sized requests: request
(User restates), search (Searcher brief), plan (Planner), work (Manager by
default; changeable with worker_agent or Planned_worker_agent, the latter
taking precedence), and ask (User reports back). Stages can be switched
off, for example with use_search false. The three reports are the work
stage's canonical context, and context is managed per stage.
Use for: bouts of work that benefit from the full lifecycle.
Not for: single bounded tasks; route those straight to the specialist.
